{"seconds": 24.662757915910333, "submission_file": "experiments/generation-repairs-v1/submissions/J08/gpt-4.1-2025-04-14/r4.json", "submission_sha256": "213af991c2651121278e17b3ce2c5d32f5bfdbee03a5e4c97d6792ceebb92b46", "code_sha256": "affe3ed6d28aace75db8ff0103c0d76ce7c6f1e1ab6f9b1310ec73b0fc74d020", "description": "This method segments puncta in fluorescence microscopy images using classical image processing and machine learning based feature learning. Workflow: (1) For training, each 128x128 patch is featurized with pixel intensities, geometric coordinates (normalized xy), local gradients, and simple multi-scale shape-texture statistics. Only labeled regions (channel1 == 1) and valid pixels (train_y >= 0) are used. For each labeled object in a patch, we extract centroid locations and create instance-specific foreground masks. A RandomForestClassifier is trained to predict background vs punctum center. A per-training-patch global threshold is grid-searched to maximize foreground F1>0.5 under synthetic instance separation (patch-wise local IDs), using only annotated pixels. At test time, each whole test image is split into 128x128 overlapping windows (step 64). Per-pixel features are extracted and pixels are classified. Patch probabilities are averaged at overlaps. Pixelwise probability map is then thresholded and postprocessed: (a) valid extent mask applied; (b) small holes and objects <4 px are removed; (c) watershed with local maxima as seeds separates instances. Final label IDs are distinct for each test image. No pixels outside valid geometric test extent are labeled. The output is a (2,727,546) numpy array of labeled images for both test images. All computation is strictly local to individual images; no smoothing/config leakage/correlation across source fields or samples. Deterministic result due to fixed seed. No specialized packages or pretrained weights are used.", "code_lines": 163, "line_limit_compliant": true, "line_limit_exclusive": 240}

